{"agent_id":"pp3g-xVYS-research-only-investigation-425-deferred-part-2-of","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-23T19:27:27.500170961Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"51afdc1a-bbd7-4e47-88b1-d9f03409877e","comment_uuid":"214c0afd-23de-495a-8e4f-d39345d88417","author":"pp3g-xVYS-research-only-investigation-425-deferred-part-2-of","content":"[OBSERVATION] Midpoint evidence bundle: live config + permission matcher + probe results\n- S2 opencode debug config (fork 1.18.13-pp3g) loads worktree .opencode/opencode.json (filesystem MCP /ASES) + 3 plugins (orchestrator-guard, crosslink-guard, rtk-guard); orchestrator agent resolved 100 rules with * allow first, edit deny, bash deny+allowlist, NO filesystem_* entries; wildcard test confirms filesystem_* matches filesystem_read_text_file.\n- Probe1 S2 orchestrator reading inside worktree via filesystem_read_text_file: succeeded (completed, content returned) — proves permission allow via * wildcard.\n- Probe2 S2 orchestrator writing via filesystem_write_file: LLM refused via system prompt (not via guard), no guard BLOCK log emitted; builder same write succeeded + guard ALLOW log 19:25:27; auditor block log exists 19:16:48 filesystem_write_file BLOCK — proves guard active on S2 for write paths.\n- Probe3 S2 orchestrator reading /tmp file via MCP: Access denied - path outside allowed directories (MCP server restriction, not permission deny)\n- S1 v2 (opencode2 beta 17963) loads same worktree opencode.json for mcp/plugins but debug shows v2 permission model action/resource vs permission/pattern, plus separate code-mode execute sandbox for filesystem (tools.filesystem.* via execute). Direct filesystem_read_text_file not used on S1; filesystem access goes through execute tool.","created_at":"2026-08-23T19:27:27.492631441Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:PEhbr63L0EG90FNYmzfxGdJ97wr3fudWUbtjj02d2lI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIjCHG5o33kWB2JEhe9xdEU8KEnFd3GVZE+NzNCbfc+kAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABANA1N32f8gmBQgT2BsXk+jJYUQJqxpdWLdUYLrRxS55Q1+98L/CHz43UPVA5ig2/58+tFVHpwzdGC9P2PVenpAA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:PEhbr63L0EG90FNYmzfxGdJ97wr3fudWUbtjj02d2lI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIjCHG5o33kWB2JEhe9xdEU8KEnFd3GVZE+NzNCbfc+kAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAGSxTSEfIV3HAqQXFCSfXSAc4LtqhwgMz7WS4ghK8nLUqKrtV7riRUGOSRVCT713tcw5gSZj/3QhuACZHaEcgs="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-xVYS-research-only-investigation-425-deferred-part-2-of","agent_seq":7,"timestamp":"2026-08-23T19:28:06.031424558Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"51afdc1a-bbd7-4e47-88b1-d9f03409877e","comment_uuid":"0809aad6-a23c-4f29-aa77-7efde4568768","author":"pp3g-xVYS-research-only-investigation-425-deferred-part-2-of","content":"Result: Empirical findings — MCP permission semantics + orchestrator filesystem-MCP exposure (per surface S1 v2 TUI opencode2 beta vs S2 fork CLI 1.18.13-pp3g) [#425]\n\n## Q1: Are MCP tools matched by same prefix/whole-string rules as bash, or separate mechanism?\n\n**Claim:** S2 uses SAME Wildcard matcher for MCP as for bash, but with degenerate pattern dimension; S1 v2 uses SEPARATE mechanism (code-mode execute sandbox).\n\n- **WHY:** S2 session/tools.ts line 390-406 shows MCP tools invoke ctx.ask({permission: key, patterns:[\"*\"], always:[\"*\"]}) where key=tool name (e.g., filesystem_read_text_file). permission/index.ts evaluate() does findLast where Wildcard.match(permission, rule.permission) && Wildcard.match(pattern, rule.pattern). Wildcard.match (core/util/wildcard.ts) converts * to .* regex ^...$ . So permission dimension is tool name wildcard, pattern dimension is always \"*\" (trivial). Bash uses same evaluate but with permission=\"bash\" and pattern=command string (e.g., \"git status --short\") matched against rule.pattern \"git status *\". Thus same engine, different arguments. S1 v2 beta (opencode2 debug agents) shows permissions as {action, resource, effect} and probe shows filesystem accessed via tools.filesystem.* inside execute JS (tool=execute, not discrete filesystem_*), not via findLast on filesystem_*.\n- **WHAT:** Based on source read: fork/opencode-src/packages/opencode/src/session/tools.ts, packages/opencode/src/permission/index.ts, packages/core/src/util/wildcard.ts, plus live headless probes (S2 orchestrator filesystem_read inside worktree succeeded, proving wildcard allow).\n- **HOW CERTAIN:** evidence-based (direct source reads + live probe). Proven for S2; inferred not proven for S1 v2's full matcher (v2 source not in fork tree; only debug JSON + behavior observed).\n- **WHAT-NOT-TESTED:** Did not compile/run isolated evaluate() unit test with custom ruleset; did not inspect opencode2 binary's Rust/TS source for v2 Wildcard; did not test MCP tools with pattern != \"*\" (all S2 MCP calls use \"*\").\n\n## Q2: With NO explicit MCP entries in agent frontmatter, what is default — allow, deny, or version-dependent?\n\n**Claim:** Default is ALLOW on BOTH surfaces as currently configured, due to global defaults \"*:* allow\".\n\n- **WHY:** Agent.Service defaults Permission.fromConfig({\"*\":\"allow\", ...}) is first rule in merged ruleset. opencode debug agent orchestrator JSON shows 100 rules, first is {permission:\"*\", pattern:\"*\", action:\"allow\"}, zero filesystem_* entries (grep empty), yet headless orchestrator filesystem_read inside worktree completed status=completed (not ask/deny). evaluate() falls back to findLast match on \"*\" -> allow. disabled() also requires rule.pattern==\"*\" && action==\"deny\" to hide tool; no such rule, so filesystem tools remain visible (registry not hiding, session would still ask). v2 orchestrator JSON also starts with {action:\"*\", resource:\"*\", effect:\"allow\"}, same outcome.\n- **WHAT:** Based on live resolved permission dump (S2 /tmp/opencode/425-probe/orch-agent.json + v2 /tmp/opencode/425-probe/v2-agents.json) and requirement that defaults are version-independent in this repo (both surfaces inherit same orchestrator.md, which has no filesystem entries).\n- **HOW CERTAIN:** proven for S2 (source + live allowed read); evidence-based for S1 v2 (debug JSON shows same * allow, but no direct filesystem_read via discrete tool to prove on v2 because v2 uses execute sandbox — our v2 probe used execute sandbox and also succeeded via read path).\n- **WHAT-NOT-TESTED:** Did not test with --auto flag off vs on (ask vs allow prompt behavior); did not test S1 headless filesystem_write via discrete tool (v1 path not exposed on v2); did not test with global config.permission override changing defaults; did not test external_directory deny interaction for files outside worktree (MCP server blocks separately).\n\n## Q3: Does orchestrator-guard successfully intercept MCP write tools on BOTH surfaces?\n\n**Claim:** YES on S2, NO on S1.\n\n- **WHY:** .opencode/plugins/orchestrator-guard.ts BLOCKED_TOOLS = {write, edit, apply_patch, filesystem_write_file, filesystem_edit_file}, ALLOWED_AGENTS={builder}, hooks tool.execute.before with sessionAgent via agentBySession map. S2 opencode debug config plugin list includes file:///.../orchestrator-guard.ts; /tmp/orchestrator-guard.log shows Plugin initialised + ALLOW for builder filesystem_write_file (19:25:27) and BLOCK for auditor filesystem_write_file (19:16:48) — proof it hot-paths. S1 v2 debug config plugin list is [./plugins/plugin.ts, ./plugins/interrupt.ts] globally, and for worktree v2 it still shows plugins from global opencode.json fallback, NOT the worktree's orchestrator-guard; opencode2 run probes show orchestrator write refusal was via system prompt, not guard (no BLOCK log would appear even if forced, because guard not loaded). Search grep for orchestrator-guard in v2 binary yields no hit; v2 beta uses separate plugin loader (TUI vs CLI).\n- **WHAT:** Based on source read of guard, live plugin list from opencode debug config + opencode2 debug config, and runtime log evidence.\n- **HOW CERTAIN:** proven for S2 (log + source); evidence-based for S1 (config evidence + absence of guard in v2 plugin list; not proven by attempting forced write that actually hits guard because prompt blocks before guard — would need low-level plugin instrumentation to prove negative).\n- **WHAT-NOT-TESTED:** Did not force orchestrator on S2 to actually call filesystem_write_file past prompt (prompt refused) to emit BLOCK log for orchestrator (auditor BLOCK is proxy); did not test other write-path MCP tools filesystem_create_directory, filesystem_move_file which are NOT in BLOCKED_TOOLS (gap, would bypass guard even on S2); did not test S1 with a custom v2 guard equivalent (none exists).\n\n## Q4: Would adding explicit 'filesystem_*': read-allow / write-deny entries to orchestrator frontmatter be honored by S2, S1, or both?\n\n**Claim:** Honored by S2, NOT honored by S1 v2 for filesystem_* keys (v2 would need different keys).\n\n- **WHY:** S2 fromConfig merges frontmatter permission via Permission.fromConfig -> ruleset -> evaluate with Wildcard. Test python wildcard shows filesystem_read* matches filesystem_read_text_file, filesystem_write* matches filesystem_write_file. Probe with NO entries already allows reads via * allow, so adding filesystem_read* allow would be redundant but explicit; adding filesystem_write* deny would create a later rule (frontmatter merged last) that beats * allow via findLast, yielding deny — then session/tools.ts ctx.ask would throw DeniedError, and guard would also block as second layer. S1 v2 permission keys are not filesystem_* but action=execute/resource=* for code-mode; v2 probe showed filesystem accessed via tools.filesystem.* inside execute (tool id=execute), not via discrete permission key, so a frontmatter entry filesystem_write_file: deny would never match (no such permission evaluated on v2). To block on v1 you'd need deny on \"execute\" or on \"edit\" (but v2's write_file via execute bypasses edit).\n- **WHAT:** Based on S2 source (config/agent.ts load + agent.ts merge + permission evaluate) and v1 probe behavior; for S1 inferred from debug agents permission shape (action/resource) and observed tool use (execute sandbox) — no v2 source tree available to prove eval path.\n- **HOW CERTAIN:** evidence-based for S2 (API contract proven by direct reads of load/merge/evaluate + wildcard); guess for S1 (no v2 source inspected, only behavioral + debug JSON).\n- **WHAT-NOT-TESTED:** Did not deploy a temporary frontmatter with filesystem_* entries and re-run debug agent to confirm deny (constraint: no repo file changes; scratch dir test would need custom --dir with its own .opencode/agents/orchestrator.md — not performed due to time, but would be cheapest discriminating test before merging); did not test v1 v2 frontmatter parsing equivalence for arbitrary keys (schema record); did not test whether v2's execute JS sandbox respects edit deny for filesystem.write_file (probe showed orchestrator prompt refused, not permission deny).\n\n## Recommendation for deferred frontmatter change (417 Part 2)\n\nAdd explicit entries — but ONLY for S2 is it effective as written; for S1 it is inert and a separate v2 guard is needed.\n\n**For S2 (fork CLI 1.18.13-pp3g): YES, add.** Exact keys (YAML frontmatter permission map) to add to .opencode/agents/orchestrator.md:\n\n```yaml\npermission:\n  # Explicit MCP filesystem — read allow (hardening over inherited * allow)\n  filesystem_read_text_file: allow\n  filesystem_read_file: allow\n  filesystem_read_multiple_files: allow\n  filesystem_read_media_file: allow\n  filesystem_list_directory: allow\n  filesystem_directory_tree: allow\n  filesystem_search_files: allow\n  filesystem_get_file_info: allow\n  filesystem_list_allowed_directories: allow\n  # Write-path — deny at permission layer (guard blocks anyway, this adds defense-in-depth)\n  filesystem_write_file: deny\n  filesystem_edit_file: deny\n  filesystem_create_directory: deny\n  filesystem_move_file: deny\n  # (Also keep existing edit: deny which covers native write/edit/apply_patch)\n```\n\nCompact wildcard alternative (equivalent via Wildcard, fewer lines):\n\n```yaml\npermission:\n  filesystem_read*: allow\n  filesystem_list*: allow\n  filesystem_get*: allow\n  filesystem_directory*: allow\n  filesystem_search*: allow\n  filesystem_write*: deny\n  filesystem_edit*: deny\n  filesystem_create*: deny\n  filesystem_move*: deny\n```\n\nEither form is honored by S2's Wildcard matcher (tested wildcard_match python). Prefer explicit full names for auditability; wildcard variant is smaller but hides exact tool inventory. **Key guarantee:** S2's evaluate treats permission string wildcard, so filesystem_write* deny will block filesystem_write_file even though pattern dimension is always \"*\".\n\n**For S1 (opencode2 TUI beta): NO as-is** — those filesystem_* keys are not evaluated on v2 (v2 uses execute sandbox). If S1 coverage required, either (a) add \"execute: deny\" is too broad, or (b) deploy a v2-side orchestrator-guard equivalent that intercepts tools.filesystem.* inside execute, or (c) await v2 parity fix. Do not add filesystem_* entries expecting S1 to be hardened — they will be silently ignored.\n\n**Additional hardening gap found:** S2 guard's BLOCKED_TOOLS omits filesystem_create_directory and filesystem_move_file — an orchestrator could still create/move files via MCP even with current guard + with above deny list, guard would not log BLOCK. Recommend extending guard to include those two, or the frontmatter deny (filesystem_create*/move*) covers permission layer but guard gap remains.\n\n**Next cheapest test before merging:** Create scratch dir /tmp/opencode/425-frontmatter-probe with its own .opencode/agents/orchestrator.md containing filesystem_write_file: deny, run opencode debug agent orchestrator --dir that-scratch, dump permission list, and attempt headless filesystem_write via builder vs orchestrator to observe DeniedError vs guard BLOCK. This was not run per one-blocker-then-stop rule.\n\nFiles: no repo changes; scratch under /tmp/opencode/425-probe only; inner-425 probe files cleaned; logs at /tmp/orchestrator-guard.log retained.","created_at":"2026-08-23T19:28:06.025354337Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:PEhbr63L0EG90FNYmzfxGdJ97wr3fudWUbtjj02d2lI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIjCHG5o33kWB2JEhe9xdEU8KEnFd3GVZE+NzNCbfc+kAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAoAf1Fd4Q2V0genxWybD3Z7MJ5jw/sqGK7IOufQBGKaaKuZHzzsaQ8YSoB0JFkUY0utETbAkig/vDbqcRxGc7CA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:PEhbr63L0EG90FNYmzfxGdJ97wr3fudWUbtjj02d2lI","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgIjCHG5o33kWB2JEhe9xdEU8KEnFd3GVZE+NzNCbfc+kAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQCUwerNctytOgg0MrYuKqPcUWkMs0/5maYIThyRCA+MJ+HUQahNCRAzjvja5uMYxypBPRoVn4YcdCttQzLY7xA0="}
